nce_check_version_drift_autoage.sql, owner-approved follow-up to INCIDENT[ref=423ecc02-fdb7-4f47-9445-d8c79d399e8c]): added scheduled_query_version_drift to the #14 auto-age category list. It is the same self-healing shape as scheduled_query_stale -- a version marker reports what a query said the LAST time it ran, and cadence_check evaluates ~05:15 UTC ahead of most of the queries it audits, so a mid-day wrapper bump raises a drift warning that heals on the next cycle. It was the one such class missing from the list AND absent from ops.alert_policy, so every version bump left a permanently-open row needing a manual UPDATE ops.alerts (embed_pending 2026-07-17/18; daily_staging_cap_check v3->v4 2026-07-27, alert 0c2b631a). A still-drifted query is re-raised by the scheduled_query_version_drift check inside that same procedure, so a genuinely unapplied wrapper cannot age away silently. No other check logic changed. v10, 2026-07-30 (bigquery/120_ci_finding_payload_detail.sql): added detail to the ci_finding alert payload so CI finding detail text reaches the operator email. v11, 2026-08-03 (bigquery/128_b3_drift_promotion.sql -- D3 MONITOR-PROMOTION SELF-FLIP): b3_trading_enabled_drift promoted WARNING->CRITICAL + joined raise_msg after state.b3_promotion_readiness fired (14 consecutive clean logged days, measured n_recent=14/n_recent_clean=14 on 2026-08-03). Promoted body copied verbatim from bigquery/79_b3_promotion.sql\'s header spec; no standalone RAISE added (the accumulator plus the single bottom RAISE handle delivery). Downstream, per bigquery/79: severity=critical for this category now folds into state.trading_enabled blocking_criticals (bigquery/78), so a real future formula clobber auto-halts new order-staging. No other check logic changed. ALSO IN v11, recorded retroactively 2026-08-06: bigquery/132_queue_driven_silence_watch.sql (commit 9a67c22, 2026-08-03) superseded bigquery/128 as the live body of this procedure and added the queue_driven_silent WARNING block (reading the new state.queue_driven_silence_watch view) plus queue_driven_silent in the #14 auto-age category list -- WITHOUT bumping this marker, so the string v11 names two different bodies historically and the DEPLOYED one is the bigquery/132 superset, not the bigquery/128 body this note described until now. That "deliberately left at v11" reasoning is now SUPERSEDED. v12, 2026-08-06 (bigquery/142_cadence_deadline_revert_and_evidence_drift.sql) -- this bump IS a real logic change, not the one-string-literal churn the prior note declined: v12 covers BOTH the bigquery/132 queue_driven_silent addition described above (previously unversioned, exactly as this note already explained) AND a new process_constant_evidence_invalidated WARNING block that bigquery/142 adds immediately after the scheduled_query_version_drift check (reads state.process_constant_evidence_drift -- a re-validation of an already-applied W5 process_reliability autotune against a later-corrected metric formula; deliberately NOT added to the #14 auto-age category list, since an invalidated-evidence finding needs human adjudication, not a self-heal). No other check logic changed. The bump-in-the-same-commit pairing enforced mechanically by scripts/check_sq_version_registry.py in CI is exactly what made this correction machine-checkable, and the same discipline governs the next change to this procedure too. v13, 2026-08-07 (audit of the 2026-08-07 daily runs, bigquery/147_run_log_content_quality.sql): adds the run_log_note_missing record-only WARNING over the new state.run_log_content_gaps view, and adds that category to the #14 auto-age allowlist. Closes a gap open since ops.run_log was created: nothing in bigquery/, scripts/ or dbt/ had ever read run_log.note or rows_written as a quality signal, so a routine could log completed with a NULL note indefinitely and every monitor stayed green. D2/2026-08-07 did exactly that -- note NULL, a 5m34s session against its 12-22min norm, and rows_written=5 logged against exactly ONE real BigQuery row (the other four were Watchlist.md ticker edits counted as though they were rows); the work itself was correct, which is why nothing caught it. Scoped to the note gap ONLY, on measurement rather than preference: over the trailing 30 days 8 of 368 terminal rows lack a note (~0.27/day), while 322 of 324 completed rows legitimately lack an instruction (it belongs on the paired started row) and 46 of 330 started rows lack one too, so an instruction check would fire ~14 percent of the time and train the operator to ignore the category. rows_written is deliberately NOT reconciled in SQL -- it has no definition the routines agree on (D2 counted repo-file edits, every other routine counts BigQuery rows), so it is pinned in prose in Claude_Task_Plan.md\'s Observability section instead. v14, 2026-08-08 (bigquery/149_cadence_check_script_version_autoage.sql): adds script_version_drift to the #14 auto-age category list. That category has no ops.alert_policy row (same shape scheduled_query_version_drift had before v9 added it here), so a script_version_drift WARNING -- self-healing, exactly like scheduled_query_version_drift -- never auto-resolved on its own; an alert sat open from 2026-08-07 until closed by hand on 2026-08-08 while the underlying condition was already clear. No other check logic changed. STAGED, NOT YET APPLIED as of 2026-08-08: bigquery/149 and this row are both currently unapplied. Do not apply this registry row until bigquery/149 is applied in the same change, or apply the procedure first -- applying only this row sets expected_version=v14 while a live v13 procedure keeps beating v13, raising a nightly scheduled_query_version_drift warning until the pair is reconciled, the same partial-apply trap noted throughout this note. v15, 2026-08-08 (bigquery/150_cadence_check_autoage_connector_and_revised.sql, live alert-board investigation -- SAME bug class as v14 above, two more instances): adds \'connector\' (OPS2\'s IBKR